# Round glmwarm: GLM-5.3-Flash's hot-list rows on the A6000 with a clean fault count, for the public table.
# In the release sitting (glm-public.card's window) all four hot:<D> rows read [cold]: 28.17 / 28.10 at depth
# 512 (11,837 / 11,842 faults) and 26.65 / 26.89 at depth 1024 (49,676 / 49,606), each count the whole
# process's. glm-public's condition took the 199.7 GB file to stay cached; the term that was wrong is our
# load's page drop (BLOOMERY_CARD_DONTNEED=1, the engine default): every ours arm drops its card trunk
# (8,966,188,280 B) and card experts (39,941,832,704 B) after uploading them. Evidence: an ours load read
# 48.9 s after an llama.cpp arm (the card set cached) and 60.9-61.4 s after an ours arm (dropped): 49.1 GB
# in 12.3 s, 4.0 GB/s [derived]. A hot arm after an id-prefix arm loaded in 77.5 / 77.6 s: 48.9 s, plus the
# upload's dropped part (the id-prefix and hot sets' overlap, 615 experts x 15.2 MB, and the trunk: 18.3 GB
# at 4.0 GB/s, 4.6 s), plus the populate of the id-prefix card experts the hot list leaves on the host
# (2,012 experts, 30.6 GB [derived: a hot list independent of the id order]) at depth-ds41.sh's populate
# rate 1.33 GB/s, 23 s: 76.5 s against 77.5 read. So hot:512's 11.8k faults are its populate's, before its
# timer, 2.6 MB a fault. hot:1024 (after hot:512 in both rounds) loaded in 100.2 / 100.9 s: 48.9 s, the
# upload of the set hot:512 dropped (49.1 GB at 4.0 GB/s, 12.3 s), and 39 s more — 52 GB at 1.33 GB/s,
# about 20k faults at 2.6 MB [derived] — which the drop does not explain: hot:512 had just populated the
# same host set, so something evicted it between the two loads. The other 30k of its 49.6k faults are
# unplaced: in its window, or a load that faults smaller. The two readings of hot:1024:
#   load   every fault before the timer; the release's 26.77 is clean, and its 5 % gap under hot:512 is
#          routing (the prompt's second 512 ids and the steps after them hit the list less), as the no-list
#          arm shows no depth cost (20.95 / 20.99; 11 latent layers x 1,536 B a position, 0.03 ms a step)
#   window 30k faults at 75.4 us = 2.3 s of its 41 s window, 5.5 %: clean hot:1024 at about hot:512's 28.1
# This run follows docs/fair-measure.md for our side (the llama.cpp arms stay llama-bench until
# lcpp-warm.sh replaces them):
#   4.2 held-out prompt. The hot list (router/glm5next-hotlist.txt) was ranked from the trace
#       router/glm5next-prose: ids 0..49,999 of glm5next/corpus-prose.ids (sha256 8af07981...bb64, 71,727
#       ids; router_trace.cpp reads the file's first --max-tokens 50000; manifest `# complete 50000 42`).
#       The prompts are ids 50,000..50,511 (d512) and 50,000..51,023 (d1024) of the same file
#       (GLM_PROSE_FROM 50000), for the no-list and the hot arms alike and for the MTP server's request;
#       each row prints `prompt ids <a>..<b>`, and a hot row inside the trace would end in [in-trace].
#   2.1 every GGUF arm runs once on its own ids, discarded (WARMUP r0), right before its round-1 row.
#   2.2 every GGUF arm opens with its host set's preheat (gguf-ranges.py; GLM_PREHEAT_K 45 for ours,
#       185.5 GB and token_embd, a superset of any plan's host set; K 36 / 37 for llama.cpp). For ours it
#       changes the timed window by 0 [derived]: the populate runs before the timer.
#   2.3 our rows count faults from generate_glm5next's `fed` record; a [cold] row prints COLD and its arm
#       runs once more; a second [cold] prints FAIL-cold and is published nowhere.
# The held-out prompt moves the hot rows down. The release's prompts (ids 0..1,023) were in the list's own
# trace; glmcard's stage 1 read the hot-67 share at 54.0 % in-sample and 48.9 % held out (39-layer mean;
# per-document drift can take it lower, 43 % as the floor). Host bytes a token 0.408 + 4.744 x (1 - h) GB:
# 2.59 GB in-sample, 2.83..3.11 GB held out, +0.24..+0.52 GB at 130-137 GB/s = +1.8..+4.0 ms a step
# [derived]. Per arm, 2 rounds [derived; the release rows' own round spread 0.1-1.5 %]:
#   512           20.3..21.5 tok/s   (20.79, 21.11: the id-prefix card set does not follow the text)
#   1024          20.3..21.5         (20.99, 21.00)
#   hot:512       25.3..27.6         (35.5 ms + 1.8..4.0 ms; its release faults were its load's)
#   hot:1024      24.2..27.6         (load reading: 37.4 ms + 1.8..4.0; window reading: hot:512's)
#   lcpp27752:512 17.3..18.3         (17.65, 17.96 [cold]; its d1024 twin, clean, 17.76 / 17.86); its row still
#                                     counts the whole process, whose load faults the card trunk and card
#                                     experts our arms dropped; the cold rule re-runs it once
#   lcpp27754mtp:512 21.5..24.5      (23.07, 23.51 on in-trace prose; the draft's acceptance moves with the text)
# Wall 31..37 min [derived], past 30: the user's approval first. The release's walls a round (ours 81-93 s,
# 1024 106-118, hot:512 102, hot:1024 145, lcpp27752 25-55, mtp 20-21: 8.6 min) three times (two rounds and
# the per-arm warm-ups), 18 preheats of 146-186 GB at 15-25 s each (the dropped 34-49 GB at 4 GB/s, the rest
# from the page cache) less the loads' own drive reads, the lease's first preheat up to 186 GB at 1.33-4
# GB/s (47-140 s), and 1.5-2.5 min for each cold re-run. The dry run's "every byte cold" line (about 1 TB a
# round) is that bound for every arm, not the estimate.
kind: baseline
question: what do GLM-5.3-Flash's hot-list rows read on the A6000 on a prompt the list never saw, warmed,
  with their fault count the timed window's, and is hot:1024's gap under hot:512 routing or faults?
term: tok/s @ n=96, depth 1024, A6000, bloomery hot:1024 (generate_glm5next, plan (a),
  BLOOMERY_HOT_LIST=router/glm5next-hotlist.txt, prose ids 50,000..51,023, held out from the list's trace of
  ids 0..49,999), faults counted from its `fed` line
unit: tok/s
predict: 24.2..27.6
condition: one lease, two rounds, the arms rotated each round, each GGUF arm warmed once on its own ids right
  before its round-1 row; BLOOMERY_PREHEAT=1, so every GGUF arm opens with its `preheat` line; each ours and
  hot row counts its faults from the `fed` line (`majflt <n> (timed, from the fed line; whole process <m>)`),
  prints `prompt ids 50000..<b>` and carries no [in-trace]; no ours or hot FAIL-cold row (a COLD row's
  re-run is the row); no ours or hot row [cpu-busy] or [other-busy]; every hot row names card_experts > 0.
  The engine runs as shipped (BLOOMERY_CARD_DONTNEED=1): after an ours arm the next ours load re-reads the
  dropped card set, 49.1 GB, about 12 s at 4.0 GB/s, before its timer and outside the timed window. The
  runner is glmwarm's (main's has neither the preheat nor the fed mark): from /Users/hckim/repo/bloomery-glmwarm, after
  generate_glm5next is built in that remote directory outside the lease,
  BLOOMERY_REMOTE='~/repo/bloomery-glmwarm' BLOOMERY_HOLD_OWNER=<the sitting's> BLOOMERY_AB_ROUNDS=2
  BLOOMERY_BOX_ENV='BLOOMERY_LEASE_CARD=docs/cards/glm-hot.card' just depth-gpu-glm5next 512 hot:512 1024
  hot:1024 lcpp27752:512 lcpp27754mtp:512 > <log> 2>&1 (GLM_PROSE_FROM is the profile's 50000)
reason: sets the hot rows of the public GLM-5.3-Flash table, which the release sitting could carry only as
  lower bounds; why now: the public table goes out with the release, and the headline needs a clean hot row
decide-in: write the four hot rows and the two no-list rows into rig-log as the public GLM rows, their
  prompt and trace id ranges beside them, with the timed counts; the ratio to llama.cpp waits for
  lcpp-warm.sh's rows on the same ids. hot:1024 / hot:512 at 0.97 or below names the depth gap routing,
  above 0.97 names the release's window faults; either way the rows are clean. A FAIL-cold ours row fails
  the condition: its faults are in the window after the populate, which no preheat reaches; the next run
  is a same-lease arm pair at BLOOMERY_HOST_LOCK=1 (the host set locked) or BLOOMERY_CARD_DONTNEED=0, not a
  rerun
decide-below: hot:1024 under 24.2: the held-out hit is under 43 %; read it from the step stats
  (BLOOMERY_STEP_STATS) and the preheat lines' seconds and GB/s beside the witness's io pressure (a
  preheat that ran into the window or evicted the host set); no row goes out before the term is named, and
  a hit that low reopens the list's corpus (a trace over more text) before the rows
decide-above: hot:1024 over 27.6: the held-out hit is above in-sample, or the release's hot:512 also lost
  time in its window; read hot:512 beside it, re-derive the host-byte term, and write the rows with the
  corrected derivation
